    "takeaway": "Three networks connected Afro-Eurasia before c.1200: the overland Silk Roads (a relay of oasis cities), the Indian Ocean (sailing the reversing monsoon winds), and the trans-Saharan caravans (unlocked by the camel saddle and Islamic commercial law). Each moved goods, but the invisible cargo mattered most: paper and other technologies, Buddhism and Islam, Champa rice, and deadly plague all traveled the same routes. Trade networks are systems that transform whole societies, not pipelines that only move goods.",
